    "gold_solution": "```python\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"JSSP\")\n    m.setParam('TimeLimit', 300)\n\n    # --- Data ---\n    n_jobs = 4\n    n_machines = 3\n    processing_times = [[11, 17, 20], [12, 14, 6], [23, 30, 23], [30, 12, 15]]\n    machine_assignments = [[0, 2, 1], [0, 1, 2], [2, 1, 0], [0, 2, 1]]\n\n    # Big-M: sum of all processing times\n    M = sum(processing_times[j][o] for j in range(n_jobs) for o in range(n_machines))\n\n    # --- Variables ---\n    # Start times for each operation\n    S = {}\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines):\n            S[(j,o)] = m.addVar(lb=0, ub=M, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Start_{j}_{o}\")\n\n    # Makespan variable\n    Cmax = m.addVar(lb=0, ub=M, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=\"Cmax\")\n\n    # Build machine -> [(job, op)] mapping\n    machine_ops = {mach: [] for mach in range(n_machines)}\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines):\n            machine_ops[machine_assignments[j][o]].append((j, o))\n\n    # Disjunctive binary variables for each pair on same machine\n    Y = {}\n    for mach in range(n_machines):\n        ops = machine_ops[mach]\n        for i in range(len(ops)):\n            for k in range(i+1, len(ops)):\n                j1, o1 = ops[i]\n                j2, o2 = ops[k]\n                Y[(j1,o1,j2,o2)] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Y_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n\n    # --- Objective: minimize makespan ---\n    m.setObjective(Cmax, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # --- Constraints ---\n    # 1. Precedence: operations within a job must follow order\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines - 1):\n            m.addConstr(\n                S[(j,o+1)] >= S[(j,o)] + processing_times[j][o],\n                name=f\"Prec_{j}_{o}\")\n\n    # 2. Disjunctive: no two operations on the same machine overlap\n    for mach in range(n_machines):\n        ops = machine_ops[mach]\n        for i in range(len(ops)):\n            for k in range(i+1, len(ops)):\n                j1, o1 = ops[i]\n                j2, o2 = ops[k]\n                p1 = processing_times[j1][o1]\n                p2 = processing_times[j2][o2]\n                y = Y[(j1,o1,j2,o2)]\n                m.addConstr(\n                    S[(j1,o1)] + p1 <= S[(j2,o2)] + M * (1 - y),\n                    name=f\"Disj_A_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n                m.addConstr(\n                    S[(j2,o2)] + p2 <= S[(j1,o1)] + M * y,\n                    name=f\"Disj_B_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n\n    # 3. Makespan: Cmax >= completion of last operation of each job\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        last_op = n_machines - 1\n        m.addConstr(\n            Cmax >= S[(j, last_op)] + processing_times[j][last_op],\n            name=f\"Makespan_J{j}\")\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Subject: Coordinated Scheduling Across Specialized Workstations\n\nContext\nOur facility operates multiple specialized machines (workstations) that support a mix of customer orders. Each job follows a defined process route made up of several operations, and the order of these operations is fixed by technical requirements. The machines are shared resources, and the way we stage and sequence work on them directly affects throughput, on-time completion, and shop-floor stability.\n\nScheduling Challenge\nWe must assign and time the operations from all jobs across the available machines in a way that avoids conflicts and maintains flow. Processing durations vary by operation, and each step must be run on a designated machine. Because these operations are linked in a chain within each job, any delay on one machine can hold up downstream steps and create idle time on others. The challenge is to coordinate these dependencies so that machines are well utilized without creating bottlenecks, and so that each job progresses steadily from start to finish.\n\nRequirements and Rules\n- Fixed process routes: Each job\u2019s operations must be completed in their defined order; no reordering is allowed.\n- Machine specificity: Each operation is assigned to a specific machine and must be performed on that machine for its known duration.\n- Machine exclusivity: A machine can process only one operation at a time.\n- No interruptions: Once an operation begins, it runs to completion without being paused or preempted.\n- Job continuity: Operations within the same job cannot overlap; a subsequent step may start only after the preceding step finishes.\n- Feasibility: All machine assignments and operation durations are known in advance and must be respected.\n\nGoal\nDevelop a single, feasible production schedule that sequences all operations on the appropriate machines so that all jobs are completed in the shortest possible total time (minimize makespan). In practical terms, this means balancing long and short tasks, reducing machine idle time, and keeping each job\u2019s workflow moving so that downstream operations are ready to start as soon as their predecessors finish.\n\nANNEX \u2013 Production Data\n- Number of jobs: 4\n- Number of machines: 3\n- Machine assignments by job and operation (fixed routing): Machine Assignments (which machine each operation uses):\n  Machine indices: 0 to 2\n  - Job 1: [Op1: Machine 0, Op2: Machine 2, Op3: Machine 1]\n  - Job 2: [Op1: Machine 0, Op2: Machine 1, Op3: Machine 2]\n  - Job 3: [Op1: Machine 2, Op2: Machine 1, Op3: Machine 0]\n  - Job 4: [Op1: Machine 0, Op2: Machine 2, Op3: Machine 1]\n- Processing times by job and operation: Processing Times (time units per operation):\n  Each job has 3 operations performed in sequence.\n  - Job 1: [Op1: 11, Op2: 17, Op3: 20]\n  - Job 2: [Op1: 12, Op2: 14, Op3: 6]\n  - Job 3: [Op1: 23, Op2: 30, Op3: 23]\n  - Job 4: [Op1: 30, Op2: 12, Op3: 15]",
